{"query":"site:github.com inurl:security/advisories \"stored cross-site scripting\" php","data":"Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via TrafficShaper description in legacy PHP firewall rules page · Advisory · opnsense/core · GitHub (https://github.com/opnsense/core/security/advisories/GHSA-m4m3-v627-wgc2)\n【turn820898search0】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week; # Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via TrafficShaper description in legacy PHP firewall rules page ... A stored cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability allows an authenticated administrator to inject arbitrary JavaScript that executes in the browser of any other administrator who opens the Firewall Rules edit page.\n\n# Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via TrafficShaper description in legacy PHP firewall rules page\n\nHigh\n\nfichtner published GHSA-m4m3-v627-wgc2 Jun 15, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nopnsense/core\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 26.1.9, <= 26.4\n\n## Patched versions\n\n26.1.10, 26.4.1\n\n## Description\n\n#### Summary\n\nA stored cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability allows an authenticated administrator to inject arbitrary JavaScript that executes in the browser of any other administrator who opens the Firewall Rules edit page. The attack vector passes through the MVC framework's TrafficShaper model — whose `description` field accepts arbitrary characters including HTML tags — and triggers in a legacy PHP page that outputs stored values without HTML escaping.\n\n#### Details\n\nThe vulnerability spans two OPNsense subsystems:\n\nInput side (MVC framework):\nThe TrafficShaper model (`src/opnsense/mvc/app/models/OPNsense/TrafficShaper/TrafficShaper.php`) defines the `description` field as a `DescriptionField`. The validation regex is `/^(.){1,255}$/`, which permits arbitrary characters including `<`, `>`, `\"`, and `'`. Payloads are stored in the OPNsense config XML without sanitization.\n\nData flow:\n`firewall_rules_edit.php:44` loads all shaper targets via the model:\n    \n    $shaper_targets = (new \\OPNsense\\TrafficShaper\\TrafficShaper())->fetchAllTargets();\n\nThe traffic shaper pipe objects — including the attacker-controlled `description` field — are passed to the template and rendered in a shaper dropdown at lines 1400 and 1412.\n\nSink (legacy PHP):\n`src/www/firewall_rules_edit.php` at lines 1400 and 1412:\n    \n    <?=$shaper['description'];?>\n\nNo `htmlspecialchars()` or any other escaping is applied. The MVC framework's global JSON escaping in `Response.php:121` (`htmlspecialchars($json, ENT_NOQUOTES)`) only protects API JSON responses and does not reach legacy PHP pages.\n\nSystemic scope:\nThe same `<?=$pconfig['...'];?>` unescaped output pattern exists in at least 16 legacy PHP files across `core` and `plugins`, all involving the `description` (or equivalent `descr`) field:\n\nCore (8 locations):\n\nFile  | Description output\n--- | ---\n`firewall_rules_edit.php:1318,1400,1412`  | Firewall rule edit — shaper dropdown\n`firewall_nat_out_edit.php:830`  | Outbound NAT edit\n`firewall_schedule_edit.php:727`  | Firewall schedule edit\n`firewall_scrub_edit.php:542`  | Scrub rule edit\n`interfaces.php:1704`  | Interface configuration\n`interfaces_ppps_edit.php:518`  | PPP edit\n`interfaces_wireless_edit.php:167`  | Wireless edit\n`system_gateway_groups_edit.php:262`  | Gateway group edit\n\nLegacy plugins (8 locations):\n\nFile  | Plugin\n--- | ---\n`services_dhcp_edit.php:371`  | ISC DHCPv4\n`services_dhcp.php:653`  | ISC DHCPv4 pool\n`services_dhcpv6_edit.php:248`  | ISC DHCPv6\n`services_rfc2136_edit.php:251`  | RFC 2136 DDNS\n`services_igmpproxy_edit.php:175`  | IGMP Proxy\n`vpn_openvpn_client.php:564`  | OpenVPN Legacy Client\n`vpn_ipsec_phase1.php:775`  | StrongSwan Legacy Phase 1\n`vpn_ipsec_phase2.php:576`  | StrongSwan Legacy Phase 2\n\n#### PoC\n\n  1. Log in to OPNsense as an administrator.\n  2. Navigate to Firewall → Shaper → Pipes.\n  3. Click Create Pipe (the \"+\" button).\n  4. In the Description field, enter:\n    \n    `<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS_PIPE')>\n    `\n\n  5. Click Save, then Apply.\n  6. Any administrator (including the attacker) who subsequently opens Firewall → Rules → Edit (any rule) will trigger the XSS: the malicious `description` is rendered unescaped in the shaper dropdown, causing `alert('XSS_PIPE')` to execute.\n\nThe vulnerability has been confirmed via Playwright automation on OPNsense 25.x with `alert('XSS_PIPE')` triggering reliably when opening `firewall_rules_edit.php`.\n\n#### Impact\n\n  * Type: Authenticated stored cross-site scripting (CWE-79).\n  * Severity: High.\n  * Affected users: All administrators who access Firewall Rules after an attacker has stored a malicious TrafficShaper description. The payload executes in the victim's browser with full access to session cookies, CSRF tokens, and the ability to perform arbitrary actions on behalf of the victim.\n  * Attack surface: The underlying pattern — unescaped `<?=?>` output in legacy PHP pages — means any description/descr field writable through MVC forms and readable through legacy PHP pages is potentially exploitable. The 16 locations listed above are the confirmed sinks; additional sinks may exist in other legacy PHP files.\n\n### Severity\n\nHigh\n\n8.1\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n/ 10\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nHigh\n\nUser interaction\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS in Multiple Management Pages · Advisory · FOGProject/fogproject · GitHub (https://github.com/FOGProject/fogproject/security/advisories/GHSA-8m2f-4x7g-p8f3)\n【turn820898search1】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; fogproject (PHP) ... The listing tables on multiple management pages (Host, Storage, Group, Image, Printer, Snapin) are vulnerable to Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), due to insufficient server-side parameter sanitization in record creations/updates and a lack of HTML escaping in listing tables. ...   * Malicious Script Execution: Deliver malicious scripts to administrators or privileged users viewing the page and run in the user browser ... Scope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\n# Stored XSS in Multiple Management Pages\n\nModerate\n\nmastacontrola published GHSA-8m2f-4x7g-p8f3 Mar 24, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nfogproject (PHP)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 1.5.10.1798\n\n## Patched versions\n\n>=1.5.10.1812,working-1.6\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nThe listing tables on multiple management pages (Host, Storage, Group, Image, Printer, Snapin) are vulnerable to Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), due to insufficient server-side parameter sanitization in record creations/updates and a lack of HTML escaping in listing tables.\n\n### Details\n\nThere are two main issues identified in the source code. The first one is in `packages/web/lib/fog/fogpage.class.php` below.\n    \n    `    private function _replaceNeeds($data)\n        {\n          ...\n          foreach ((array)$arrayReplace as $name => &$val) {\n                $this->dataFind[] = sprintf(\n                    '${%s}',\n                    $name\n                );\n                $val = trim($val);\n                $this->dataReplace[] = $val; // Here no HTML escaping to filter out high-risk HTML entities (e.g., <script>)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored Cross-Site Scripting via Unescaped SNMP Data in Multiple Legacy Pages · Advisory · librenms/librenms · GitHub (https://github.com/librenms/librenms/security/advisories/GHSA-v5jp-f342-234h)\n【turn820898search2】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Security Advisory: Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Unescaped SNMP Data in Multiple Legacy Pages ... LibreNMS is vulnerable to stored cross-site scripting (XSS) through multiple SNMP-sourced data fields that are rendered without HTML escaping in legacy PHP template pages.\n\n# Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Unescaped SNMP Data in Multiple Legacy Pages\n\nModerate\n\nmurrant published GHSA-v5jp-f342-234h Aug 4, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nlibrenms/librenms (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 26.2.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\nNone\n\n## Description\n\n# Security Advisory: Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Unescaped SNMP Data in Multiple Legacy Pages\n\n## Summary\n\nLibreNMS is vulnerable to stored cross-site scripting (XSS) through multiple SNMP-sourced data fields that are rendered without HTML escaping in legacy PHP template pages. An attacker with device management access (or network access to place a rogue SNMP device that gets enrolled) can inject crafted values that execute arbitrary JavaScript when any LibreNMS admin views the affected pages.\n\nThe vulnerability was confirmed with data exfiltration of SNMP community strings and CSRF tokens from the admin's browser.\n\n## Affected Product\n\n  * Ecosystem: Composer\n  * Package: `librenms/librenms`\n  * Affected versions: <= 26.2.0\n  * Patched versions: None\n\n#### 4. VRF Name, `includes/html/pages/routing/vrf.inc.php` (line 115)\n    \n    echo $vrf['vrf_name'] . '</a><br />';\n\n#### 5. VRF Description, `includes/html/pages/routing/vrf.inc.php` (line 116)\n    \n    echo '<span class=box-desc>' . $vrf['mplsVpnVrfDescription'] . '</span></td>';\n\n#### 6. VRF Route Distinguisher, `includes/html/pages/routing/vrf.inc.php` (line 117)\n    \n    echo '<td width=100 class=box-desc>' . $vrf['mplsVpnVrfRouteDistinguisher'] . '</td>';\n\n#### 7. Process User, `includes/html/pages/device/processes.inc.php` (line 88)\n    \n    echo '<td>' . $entry['user'] . '</td>';\n\n#### 8. Process Command, `includes/html/pages/device/processes.inc.php` (line 89)\n    \n    echo '<td>' . $entry['command'] . '</td>';\n\n#### 9. SLA Tag and Owner, `includes/html/pages/device/slas.inc.php` (line 20)\n    \n    $name = 'SLA #' . $sla['sla_nr'] . ' - ' . trans_fb(...);\n    if ($sla['tag']) { $name .= ': ' . $sla['tag']; }\n    if ($sla['owner']) { $name .= ' (Owner: ' . $sla['owner'] . ')'; }\n    echo \"<div class=\\\"well well-sm\\\"><h3 class=\\\"panel-title\\\">$name</h3></div>\";\n\n### Root Cause\n\nAll affected files are legacy PHP templates in `includes/html/pages/` that predate LibreNMS's migration to Laravel Blade templates. The modern Blade templates properly use `{{ }}` (auto-escaped) syntax, but these legacy pages use raw `echo` without `htmlspecialchars()` or `htmlentities()`.\n\nThe data in all cases originates from SNMP polling of network devices and is stored in the database without sanitization. When rendered in the legacy pages, the SNMP-sourced values pass directly to the HTML output.\n\n## Suggested Remediation\n\nApply `htmlspecialchars()` to all SNMP-sourced data output in legacy PHP templates:\n    \n    // Before (vulnerable):\n    echo '<td>' . $peer['bgpPeerDescr'] . \"</td>\";\n    \n    // After (fixed):\n    echo '<td>' . htmlspecialchars($peer['bgpPeerDescr']) . \"</td>\";\n\nAll affected locations requiring the same fix:\n\nFile  | Line  | Field\n--- | --- | ---\n`includes/html/pages/routing/bgp.inc.php`  | 329  | `$peer['bgpPeerDescr']`\n`includes/html/pages/device/routing/bgp.inc.php`  | 241  | `$peer['bgpPeerDescr']`\n`includes/html/pages/device/routing/bgp.inc.php`  | 230  | `$peer['bgpPeerLastErrorText']`\n`includes/html/pages/routing/vrf.inc.php`  | 115  | `$vrf['vrf_name']`\n`includes/html/pages/routing/vrf.inc.php`  | 116  | `$vrf['mplsVpnVrfDescription']`\n`includes/html/pages/routing/vrf.inc.php`  | 117  | `$vrf['mplsVpnVrfRouteDistinguisher']`\n`includes/html/pages/device/processes.inc.php`  | 88  | `$entry['user']`\n`includes/html/pages/device/processes.inc.php`  | 89  | `$entry['command']`\n`includes/html/pages/device/slas.inc.php`  | 20  | `$sla['tag']`, `$sla['owner']`\n\nAdditionally, a broader audit of all legacy PHP templates in `includes/html/pages/` is recommended, as additional unescaped SNMP-sourced fields may exist beyond those identified here.\n\n## Timeline\n\n  * 2026-03-15: Vulnerability discovered and PoC confirmed on LibreNMS 26.2.0 (Docker)\n  * 2026-03-15: Vendor notified via GitHub Security Advisory\n  * 2026-03-20: Updated GitHub Security Advisory\n\n## Credits\n\n  * Discovered by: Dimas Herjunodarpito Notoprayitno (@dimashn04)\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n5.8\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecurity Advisories · nocodb/nocodb · GitHub (https://github.com/nocodb/nocodb/security/advisories)\n【turn820898search3】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; ## Security Advisories ...   * Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Attachment MIME Type\n\n# Security: nocodb/nocodb\n\n## Security and quality\n\n  * Overview\n  *   * ### Reporting\n\n    * Security policy\n    * Advisories\n\n## Security Advisories\n\nView known security vulnerabilities and report new vulnerabilities privately to maintainers.\n\nReport a vulnerability\n\n  * Denial of Service via Unhandled Metadata Query Error\n\nGHSA-gcxw-8q43-jhjc published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nModerate\n\n  * IP Address Spoofing via Unconditional Proxy Trust\n\nGHSA-2rm5-wf7w-8j57 published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nModerate\n\n  * Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Attachment MIME Type\n\nGHSA-3f7w-wqf7-786q published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nHigh\n\n  * Instance Metadata Disclosure via Aggregated Meta Endpoint\n\nGHSA-5892-3964-gc52 published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nModerate\n\n  * TLS Client Private Key Exposure via Data Source Read\n\nGHSA-rjc4-j36j-gjjc published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nHigh\n\n  * Uncontrolled Resource Consumption via Bulk Record Endpoints\n\nGHSA-mmvm-m83w-6684 published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nModerate\n\n  * Privilege Escalation via Incomplete Admin Bootstrap\n\nGHSA-mjxv-r6j7-m8gv published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nHigh\n\n  * Stored Cross-Site Scripting via Table Deletion Dialog\n\nGHSA-638g-xw92-xq9x published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nHigh\n\n  * Insecure Direct Object Reference in Row Comment Operations\n\nGHSA-x3xv-qpw2-33x6 published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nHigh\n\n  * Client Authentication Bypass in OAuth Token Exchange\n\nGHSA-4cx5-q9xx-5pgj published Oct 27, 2026 by mertmit\n\nHigh\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored cross-site scripting in `wpautop()` allows an unauthenticated visitor to inject script · Advisory · WordPress/wordpress-develop · GitHub (https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/security/advisories/GHSA-qg7r-fjh2-wvx8)\n【turn820898search4】 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; # Stored cross-site scripting in `wpautop()` allows an unauthenticated visitor to inject script\n\n# Stored cross-site scripting in `wpautop()` allows an unauthenticated visitor to inject script\n\nHigh\n\naaronjorbin published GHSA-qg7r-fjh2-wvx8 Sep 18, 2026\n\n## Software\n\nWordPress\n\n## Affected versions\n\n7.1.0 - 7.1\n\n7.0.0 - 7.0.4\n\n6.9.0 - 6.9.7\n\n6.8.0 - 6.8.8\n\n6.7.0 - 6.7.7\n\n6.6.0 - 6.6.7\n\n6.5.0 - 6.5.10\n\n6.4.0 - 6.4.10\n\n6.3.0 - 6.3.10\n\n6.2.0 - 6.2.11\n\n6.1.0 - 6.1.12\n\n6.0.0 - 6.0.14\n\n5.9.0 - 5.9.16\n\n5.8.0 - 5.8.15\n\n5.7.0 - 5.7.17\n\n5.6.0 - 5.6.19\n\n5.5.0 - 5.5.20\n\n5.4.0 - 5.4.21\n\n5.3.0 - 5.3.23\n\n5.2.0 - 5.2.26\n\n5.1.0 - 5.1.24\n\n5.0.0 - 5.0.27\n\n4.9.0 - 4.9.31\n\n4.8.0 - 4.8.30\n\n4.7.0 - 4.7.35\n\n## Patched versions\n\n7.1.1\n\n7.0.5\n\n6.9.8\n\n6.8.9\n\n6.7.8\n\n6.6.8\n\n6.5.11\n\n6.4.11\n\n6.3.11\n\n6.2.12\n\n6.1.13\n\n6.0.15\n\n5.9.17\n\n5.8.16\n\n5.7.18\n\n5.6.20\n\n5.5.21\n\n5.4.22\n\n5.3.24\n\n5.2.27\n\n5.1.25\n\n5.0.28\n\n4.9.32\n\n4.8.31\n\n4.7.36\n\n## Description\n\nAn unauthenticated visitor can post a comment containing a KSES-legal element whose attribute value holds a newline. `wpautop()` treats that newline as a paragraph boundary and rewrites a `<p>` inside the element, ultimately resulting in a live event-handler attribute being rendered on the page.\n\nWordPress 7.1.1 has been released containing a fix for the vulnerability, and as a courtesy to users on older branches the fix has been backported to all branches back to 5.9.\n\nDiscovered and responsibly disclosed by Rafie Muhammad (Awesome Motive, Inc.)\n\n### Severity\n\nHigh\n\n### CVE ID\n\nNo known CVE\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecurity Advisories · WordPress/wordpress-develop · GitHub (https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/security/advisories)\n【turn820898search5】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; ## Security Advisories ...   * A Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in the labels of the Comments Pagination and Post Navigation Link blocks\n\n## Security Advisories\n\nView information about security vulnerabilities from this repository's maintainers.\n\n  * Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) and denial of service issues in Interactivity API bindings\n\nGHSA-83c2-c4pc-wmp3 published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nHigh\n\n  * A hardening fix for unsafe preg_replace() replacements in the Image and Playlist blocks\n\nGHSA-2p22-wv7j-cvfv published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nModerate\n\n  * A cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in the Categories block via format string injection\n\nGHSA-7q86-3j97-744j published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nModerate\n\n  * An issue allowing post content to shadow Script Module data with a matching element ID\n\nGHSA-wjwj-p342-9fq7 published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nModerate\n\n  * A Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in the labels of the Comments Pagination and Post Navigation Link blocks\n\nGHSA-j255-3x8q-7q7g published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nModerate\n\n  * A Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in Customizer selective refresh\n\nGHSA-9vrg-x932-q57q published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nModerate\n\n  * A race condition during core updates that could lead to remote code execution\n\nGHSA-4w3m-c66w-69mg published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nLow\n\n  * An information disclosure issue exposing unpublished Customizer changes to unauthenticated visitors\n\nGHSA-p2r6-rwjp-phv3 published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nModerate\n\n  * A permissions issue letting custom roles that moderate comments read, edit and delete notes on posts they can't edit\n\nGHSA-vchg-92fx-636p published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nLow\n\n  * An Editor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in the personal data erasure requests screen\n\nGHSA-mqjw-vvgg-xfvf published Oct 29, 2026 by aaronjorbin\n\nLow\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDuplicate Advisory: phpMyFAQ: Stored XSS via Utils::parseUrl() in comment rendering · GHSA-w42g-jj8w-fj77 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-w42g-jj8w-fj77)\n【turn820898search6】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; phpMyFAQ before 4.1.2 contains a stored cross-site scripting vulnerability in Utils::parseUrl() that allows authenticated users to inject JavaScript via malformed URLs in comments. ...   * https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/phpmyfaq-stored-xss-via-utils-parseurl-in-comment-rendering\n\n## Duplicate Advisory: phpMyFAQ: Stored XSS via Utils::parseUrl() in comment rendering\n\nHigh severity GitHub Reviewed Published May 15, 2026 to the GitHub Advisory Database • Updated Jun 8, 2026\n\nWithdrawn This advisory was withdrawn on Jun 8, 2026\n\nVulnerability details Dependabot alerts 0\n\n## Package\n\nphpMyFAQ/phpMyFAQ (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 4.1.2\n\n## Patched versions\n\n4.1.2\n\nthorsten/phpmyfaq (Composer)\n\n< 4.1.2\n\n4.1.2\n\n## Description\n\n### Duplicate Advisory\n\nThis advisory has been withdrawn because it is a duplicate of GHSA-9525-27vj-c8r8. This link is maintained to preserve external references.\n\n### Original Description\n\nphpMyFAQ before 4.1.2 contains a stored cross-site scripting vulnerability in Utils::parseUrl() that allows authenticated users to inject JavaScript via malformed URLs in comments. Attackers can craft URLs with unescaped quotes to inject event handlers, stealing admin session cookies and achieving full application takeover when visitors view affected FAQ pages.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in Customizer selective refresh · Advisory · WordPress/wordpress-develop · GitHub (https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/security/advisories/GHSA-9vrg-x932-q57q)\n【turn820898search7】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 days ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; # A Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in Customizer selective refresh ... A user with the Contributor role or above can store markup in post content that Customizer selective refresh treats as HTML, which runs a script in an Administrator's Customizer preview.\n\n# A Contributor+ stored cross-site scripting (XSS) issue in Customizer selective refresh\n\nModerate\n\naaronjorbin published GHSA-9vrg-x932-q57q Oct 29, 2026\n\n## Software\n\nWordPress\n\n## Affected versions\n\n7.1.0 - 7.1.3\n\n7.0.0 - 7.0.7\n\n6.9.0 - 6.9.10\n\n6.8.0 - 6.8.11\n\n6.7.0 - 6.7.10\n\n6.6.0 - 6.6.10\n\n6.5.0 - 6.5.13\n\n6.4.0 - 6.4.13\n\n6.3.0 - 6.3.13\n\n6.2.0 - 6.2.14\n\n6.1.0 - 6.1.15\n\n6.0.0 - 6.0.17\n\n5.9.0 - 5.9.19\n\n5.8.0 - 5.8.18\n\n5.7.0 - 5.7.20\n\n5.6.0 - 5.6.22\n\n5.5.0 - 5.5.23\n\n5.4.0 - 5.4.24\n\n5.3.0 - 5.3.26\n\n5.2.0 - 5.2.29\n\n5.1.0 - 5.1.27\n\n5.0.0 - 5.0.30\n\n## Patched versions\n\n7.1.4\n\n7.0.8\n\n6.9.11\n\n6.8.12\n\n6.7.11\n\n6.6.11\n\n6.5.14\n\n6.4.14\n\n6.3.14\n\n6.2.15\n\n6.1.16\n\n6.0.18\n\n5.9.20\n\n5.8.19\n\n5.7.21\n\n5.6.23\n\n5.5.24\n\n5.4.25\n\n5.3.27\n\n5.2.30\n\n5.1.28\n\n5.0.31\n\n## Description\n\nA user with the Contributor role or above can store markup in post content that Customizer selective refresh treats as HTML, which runs a script in an Administrator's Customizer preview.\n\nWordPress 7.1.4 has been released containing a fix for the vulnerability, and as a courtesy to users on older branches the fix has been backported to all branches back to 5.0.\n\nDiscovered and responsibly disclosed by Rafie Muhammad (Awesome Motive, Inc.)\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n### CVE ID\n\nNo known CVE\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecurity Advisories · statamic/cms · GitHub (https://github.com/statamic/cms/security/advisories)\n【turn820898search8】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ## Security Advisories ...   * Stored cross-site scripting via SVG assets that survive upload sanitization\n\n# Security: statamic/cms\n\n## Security and quality\n\n  * Overview\n  *   * ### Reporting\n\n    * Security policy\n    * Advisories\n\n## Security Advisories\n\nView known security vulnerabilities and report new vulnerabilities privately to maintainers.\n\n  * Impersonation could be started when disabled via configuration\n\nGHSA-jh5q-3472-hwjp published Oct 6, 2026 by jasonvarga\n\nModerate\n\n  * Server-Side Request Forgery via Glide (IPv6 address translation)\n\nGHSA-j2q4-vcj5-v3h3 published Oct 6, 2026 by jasonvarga\n\nLow\n\n  * Arbitrary class instantiation via user-supplied validation rules in the Control Panel\n\nGHSA-p5xm-f98m-55rw published Oct 6, 2026 by jasonvarga\n\nHigh\n\n  * IP address protection scheme bypassable via a forwarded header\n\nGHSA-wj57-j7rq-v647 published Sep 16, 2026 by jasonvarga\n\nModerate\n\n  * User enumeration via timing discrepancy in login\n\nGHSA-qcj3-3rx2-4c2j published Sep 16, 2026 by jasonvarga\n\nModerate\n\n  * Stored cross-site 