icate Advisory\n\nThis advisory has been withdrawn because it is a duplicate of GHSA-f5p7-2c9q-8896. This link is maintained to preserve external references.\n\n### Original Description\n\nphpMyFAQ before 4.1.2 contains a stored cross-site scripting vulnerability in FAQ creation and update endpoints that bypass sanitization through encode-decode cycles. The vulnerability allows authenticated attackers with FAQ_ADD permission to inject malicious script tags via question or answer parameters, which execute in every visitor's browser when FAQ content is rendered with the raw Twig filter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecurity Advisories · mautic/mautic · GitHub (https://github.com/mautic/mautic/security/advisories)\n【turn820898search10】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ## Security Advisories ...   * Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in Project Option Selector\n\nHigh\n\n  * Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in Projects Component\n\nGHSA-7h65-whp7-rgqf published May 29, 2026 by patrykgruszka\n\nHigh\n\n  * Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) in Theme Templates\n\nGHSA-9fx4-7cmj-47vg published May 29, 2026 by patrykgruszka\n\nCritical\n\n  * Path Traversal via Campaign Import\n\nGHSA-6r9h-4h75-7q4x published May 29, 2026 by patrykgruszka\n\nCritical\n\n  * SQL Injection in API Contact Filtering\n\nGHSA-fcmw-wx57-9p75 published May 29, 2026 by patrykgruszka\n\nHigh\n\n  * SQL Injection in Contact Activity API Sorting\n\nGHSA-r5j5-q42h-fc93 published Feb 24, 2026 by RCheesley\n\nHigh\n\n  * SSRF in the Mautic Focus component\n\nGHSA-jmv8-8j9j-rcpc published May 29, 2026 by patrykgruszka\n\nModerate\n\n  * Mautic user without privileged access to the Marketplace can install and uninstall composer packages\n\nGHSA-3fq7-c5m8-g86x published Dec 2, 2025 by RCheesley\n\nCritical\n\n  * GrapesJsBuilder File Upload allows all file uploads\n\nGHSA-5xw2-57jx-pgjp published Dec 2, 2025 by RCheesley\n\nHigh\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS in site name rendered without escaping · Advisory · craftcms/cms · GitHub (https://github.com/craftcms/cms/security/advisories/GHSA-5fjj-496j-2qqf)\n【turn820898search11】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; A stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the site name field.\n\n# Stored XSS in site name rendered without escaping\n\nLow\n\nangrybrad published GHSA-5fjj-496j-2qqf Aug 18, 2026\n\n## Software\n\ncraftcms/cms\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 5.0.0-RC1, < 5.10.11\n\n## Patched versions\n\n5.10.11\n\n## Description\n\nA stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the site name field. The application fails to sanitize input, allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary JavaScript when another user views a page with the malicious table field.\n\n## Required Permissions\n\n  * Administrator account\n  * `allowAdminChanges` must be enabled.\n\n### Steps to Reproduce\n\n  1. Log in to the CP as admin.\n  2. Settings > Sites > create or rename a site. Set the Name to any XSS payload.\n  3. Navigate to Settings > Email & Notice the XSS execution.\n\n## Impact\n\nStored XSS in the control panel.\n\nReport ID: 1511\n\n### Severity\n\nLow\n\n### CVE ID\n\nNo known CVE\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * mHe4am Reporter"}
